Backport proot-me/proot d8bf141 (missing third hunk): drop the
chained-syscall bookkeeping from handle_ptracee_event().

fix-restart-syscall-no-chain.patch rewrote restart_original_syscall()
to reset the registers and the trap in place.  It no longer calls
register_chained_syscall(), so it no longer creates the chain.syscalls
entry that the old implementation guaranteed to exist.

handle_ptracee_event() still relied on that side effect: when
update_wait_status() returns 0 -- which it does from the
WIFEXITED/WIFSIGNALED branch that calls restart_original_syscall() --
it called chain_next_syscall(ptracer) unconditionally.  That call
asserts chain.syscalls != NULL and then forges yet another syscall on
top of the registers and instruction pointer that
restart_original_syscall() has just reset, so the ptracer resumes at a
stale address.  Upstream removed the call in the same commit precisely
because the rewrite makes it both redundant and wrong.

Reached by any ptracer whose child exits while it is inside
wait4()/waitpid(), for instance login-inner waiting for
nixos-rebuild to finish a switch.

diff --git a/src/ptrace/wait.c b/src/ptrace/wait.c
index 80172d7..973a7d8 100644
--- a/src/ptrace/wait.c
+++ b/src/ptrace/wait.c
@@ -382,9 +382,7 @@
 		int status;
 
 		status = update_wait_status(ptracer, ptracee);
-		if (status == 0)
-			chain_next_syscall(ptracer);
-		else
+		if (status != 0)
 			poke_reg(ptracer, SYSARG_RESULT, (word_t) status);
 
 		/* Write ptracer's register cache back.  */
